## Deals that have stopped moving

Each stage can have a follow-up limit: **Chase after … days without movement** (see [Stages and vocabularies](/en/crm/stages/#pipeline-stages)). When a deal stays in its stage longer than that, it is overdue for follow-up.

Every morning at 10:00 Singapore time, Operon checks all open deals. For each overdue deal:

- **An e-mail** goes to the deal's person in charge, or to its Manager in charge if there is no person in charge. It says how many days the deal has been in its stage. At most one e-mail is sent per deal per day. The e-mail is currently written in Chinese.
- The deal appears under **Stalled leads** in **My Week** of the person, as "Stuck in … for …d".
- The deal is listed under **Follow-ups** on the [Dashboard](/en/crm/dashboard/).

Moving the deal to another stage — with a note on what happened — stops the reminders.

## Other notifications

| When | Who is told | How |
|---|---|---|
| Someone is made the Manager in charge of an opportunity, when it is created or later | That manager (not the person who made the change) | In **Triage**; by e-mail too if they have turned it on (see [Personal settings](/en/getting-started/settings-help/#your-settings)). |
| A contract is waiting for approval | The approver | See [6 · Approval](/en/crm/approval/). |

## Asking an AI employee about the pipeline

Managers and tenant admins with CRM access can ask Lily, the secretary AI employee, about the pipeline in chat — for example, how many deals are in each stage, or which deals a person has open. She reads the opportunities you can see; she does not change them.

The sales AI employee does not run workflows yet: it switches on the CRM suite for your firm.
